data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","item":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"toolu_bdrk_01U6yanCkuPPao88F2pDGTzY","output":"  1  ---\n  2  name: report-builder-v2\n  3  description: Builds an interactive, filterable HTML report (PowerBI-style) from a compact JSON ReportConfig via the build_report tool. Use when the user asks for a report, a báo cáo, an executive summary as a document, a dashboard-style HTML deliverable, or a shareable link to analysis results.\n  4  ---\n  5  \n  6  # Report Builder v2\n  7  \n  8  You write a **JSON `ReportConfig`**; `build_report` renders it into a self-contained\n  9  interactive report (sidebar, slicers, cross-filtering charts, RAG, sortable tables,\n 10  light/dark themes). The template owns all HTML/CSS/JS and every colour — you never write\n 11  markup and never choose a theme. Your job is content, data and relationships.\n 12  \n 13  ## Workflow\n 14  \n 15  1. **Open a preset in `assets/examples/` and read it fully** — it is your template.\n 16     - `performance.json` — segment P&L. Two facts by reference out of one evidence, split\n 17       by `where`. One slicer, `insight` · `subtitle` · `kpi_group` · `driver_row` · `chart` ·\n 18       `chart_pair` · `table` (fact-driven) · `actions`, RAG. Start here.\n 19     - `campaign.json` — marketing, and the one config holding **both** fact shapes: Genie's\n 20       channel table by reference, a hand-composed monthly table inline. Adds `divider`, an\n 21       `afb_line` with `forecast_band`, and a `table` in explicit `rows` mode.\n 22     - `segment_scenario.json` — a long fact (`scenario` column: Actual/Budget/SPLY) with a\n 23       declaration-only dimension. Copy this one whenever your data has a scenario or\n 24       metric column rather than one column per scenario.\n 25  2. **Point `data.facts` at the evidence the sub-agent saved** — `{\"from\": \"wi_…\"}`, not the\n 26     rows. See *Data*.\n 27  3. **Work out what the report says with `compute_measures`, not in your head** — see\n 28     *Getting the numbers*.\n 29  4. **Declare a measure for every figure you will show**, then bind them with `{{measure}}`.\n 30  5. **Write the config with `write_file` to `/report_config.json`, then call\n 31     `build_report(config_path=\"/report_config.json\", filename=…)`.**\n 32  \n 33  Adapt the preset and send it. `build_report` validates before rendering and returns **every**\n 34  problem it found in one message, each with its location — so submitting a config is how you\n 35  check it. Re-reading your own JSON to be certain first costs more than the call does, and\n 36  finds less.\n 37  \n 38  ### Two stages, when one emission would be too big\n 39  \n 40  A whole config is one `write_file` argument, and a large one has been cut off mid-JSON by\n 41  the 64,000-token output ceiling — nothing saved, nothing to edit, the whole emission lost.\n 42  Splitting the submission avoids that, and gets the half that carries the numbers checked\n 43  before you write the half that shows them.\n 44  \n 45  Pick a short `session` name (`cibg-h1`), then:\n 46  \n 47  1. `write_file` the **semantic model** — `meta`, `data`, `measures`, `filters`, and nothing\n 48     else — to `/report-sessions/<session>/model.json`. That exact path is where the model is\n 49     sealed and where stage 2 looks for it; it is also what survives a compaction.\n 50  2. `build_report(stage=\"model\", session=\"<session>\", config_path=\"/report-sessions/<session>/model.json\")`.\n 51     On success it hands back every identifier stage 2 may bind to. Do not start writing\n 52     sections until this passes — they bind to it.\n 53  3. `write_file` the **presentation** — `sections`, plus `conditional_formats` and\n 54     `format_defaults` if you use them — to a file of your choosing.\n 55  4. `build_report(stage=\"sections\", session=\"<session>\", config_path=…, filename=…)`. This\n 56     builds the report.\n 57  \n 58  Stage 2 takes every section at once: there is no per-section call, because a section is only\n 59  checkable against the sections around it. Stage 2 may reference only what the sealed model\n 60  declares — a fact, measure or dimension that is not in it is rejected by name.\n 61  \n 62  Two stages do not buy two budgets: the three-rejection rule below counts the report, not the\n 63  stage. And the one-shot route is unchanged — omit `stage` and send the whole config when it\n 64  is small enough to emit safely.\n 65  \n 66  ## The contract\n 67  \n 68  Every object below is closed: a key that is not on this card is rejected, and the error\n 69  prints the allowed list for that exact path. Guessing is therefore never cheaper than\n 70  looking — the card is the whole contract, and `contracts/report-config.schema.json` says\n 71  the same thing in 29 KB with no lookup order.\n 72  \n 73  Read it as: `key*` required · after `|` optional · `∈` the complete set of allowed values ·\n 74  `⊕` two exclusive shapes · `→` the key that tells the union's members apart.\n 75  \n 76  ```text\n 77  config                meta* data* sections* | measures filters conditional_formats\n 78  \n 79  meta                  title* | label doc_title meta_line lang brand footer\n 80  data                  facts* | dimensions\n 81    facts{}             from* | where columns\n 82    dimensions{}        | label keys\n 83  filters[]             dim* | header\n 84  measures{}            one shape per measure:\n 85    aggregate           agg* fact* col* | where format\n 86    expr                expr* | format\n 87    const               const* from* | format\n 88                        agg ∈ sum avg min max count last first\n 89  conditional_formats{} thresholds* | target else_class\n 90    thresholds[]        op* value* class* | value2\n 91                        op ∈ lt lte gt gte eq between\n 92                        class, else_class ∈ good warn bad up down neutral\n 93  sections[]            id* title* blocks* | label in_nav nav_level ∈ 0 1\n 94  \n 95  blocks[] → type\n 96    insight             type* html*\n 97    subtitle            type* text* | margin_top\n 98    kpi_group           type* cards* | columns ∈ 2 3\n 99      cards[]           label* value* | unit sub cf delta progress\n100        delta           text* | dir ∈ positive negative neutral\n101        progress        | width state ∈ good warn bad   ⊕   ratio | cap rule\n102    driver_row          type* boxes*                                    boxes ≤ 4\n103      boxes[]           val* lbl* | delta\n104    chart               type* id* spec* | title sub size ∈ sm md lg\n105    chart_pair          type* charts*                                   charts = 2\n106    table               type* columns* | fact rows dim total sortable variant ∈ default product\n107      columns[]         | label col align ∈ left right center format cf\n108      rows[]            key* cells*\n109        cells[]         v* | bold align tag ∈ up down warn neutral cf   ⊕   a string\n110      total             cells*\n111    actions             type* cards*\n112      cards[]           priority* title* body* | action_id      priority ∈ high medium\n113        body[]          label* | html items signal              signal: text* | label\n114    divider             type*\n115  \n116  spec → kind\n117    bar_group | line    kind* x* series* | y_name value_suffix legend\n118      x                 from_fact* col*\n119      series[]          from* | name type ∈ bar line line_style ∈ solid dashed dotted label\n120        from            fact* col* | agg where\n121    pie                 kind* slices* | radius value_suffix legend\n122      slices            from_fact* name_col* value_col*\n123    afb_line            kind* months* actual* forecast* budget* | forecast_band y_name\n124      actual            from*\n125      forecast          from*\n126      budget            from*\n127        from            fact* col* | month_col where\n128  \n129  format                int ty0 ty1 ty2 pct0 pct1 pct2 signed_ty0 signed_ty1 usd_mn kh raw\n130  ```\n131  \n132  Four things the card states and that are worth reading twice, because each one has cost a\n133  whole build:\n134  \n135  **Two unions, two different discriminators.** A **block** says `type`; a **chart spec** says\n136  `kind`. Writing `kind` on a block fails at every block in the config at once.\n137  \n138  **A name that fits one level up is still a rejected key.** `title` belongs to a section, a\n139  chart block and an action card — and to nothing else, so a `kpi_group` or a `table` has no\n140  title. `text` is `subtitle`'s and `delta`'s; `insight` takes `html`. `items` is an action\n141  body's; `kpi_group` and `actions` take `cards`. A section has no `subtitle` key — `subtitle`\n142  is a block you put in its `blocks`.\n143  \n144  **The card names every key there is.** When a build rejects one, the fix is on the card, not\n145  in a second guess: `change` on a kpi card is `delta: {text, dir}`; `value`/`max` on a\n146  `progress` is `ratio`/`cap`; `min`/`max`/`color` on a threshold is `op`/`value`/`class`; a\n147  format you compose yourself (`n0`) is `int`.\n148  \n149  **`format` is display only and never rescales.** A count — customers, transactions,\n150  headcount — is `int`.\n151  \n152  The card gives the keys; this gives the nesting. It is the smallest config that builds:\n153  \n154  ```json config\n155  { \"meta\": { \"label\": \"TECHCOMBANK · CIBG\", \"title\": \"CIBG — TOI 6T2026\",\n156              \"meta_line\": \"6T2026 · nguồn: Genie · BF Hub\" },\n157    \"data\": {\n158      \"dimensions\": { \"sector\": { \"label\": \"Sector\", \"keys\": [\"re\", \"infra\"] } },\n159      \"facts\": { \"sector_toi\": { \"from\": \"wi_cibg_a4c8c2c1\",\n160                                 \"where\": { \"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\" } } } },\n161    \"measures\": { \"toi_act\": { \"agg\": \"sum\", \"fact\": \"sector_toi\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n162                               \"format\": \"ty1\" } },\n163    \"filters\": [ { \"dim\": \"sector\" } ],\n164    \"sections\": [ { \"id\": \"overview\", \"label\": \"01 — Tổng quan\", \"title\": \"Tổng quan CIBG\",\n165                    \"blocks\": [ { \"type\": \"insight\",\n166                                  \"html\": \"TOI đạt <strong>{{toi_act|ty1}} tỷ</strong>.\" } ] } ] }\n167  ```\n168  \n169  ## Data\n170  \n171  `data.facts` maps `fact_id → table`, and a table has **two shapes — the shape is where the\n172  numbers came from**:\n173  \n174  | Shape | Write it as | When |\n175  |---|---|---|\n176  | **by reference** (default) | `{ \"from\": \"wi_…\", \"where\": {…}?, \"columns\": […]? }` | the numbers came from Genie — i.e. nearly always |\n177  | inline rows | `[ {col: value, …}, … ]` | only a table you composed yourself: an action plan, a footnote |\n178  \n179  By reference, the engine opens the evidence and takes the rows itself. Nothing is retyped,\n180  so nothing can be mistyped — and a fact you point at costs a line of config instead of two\n181  hundred. `where` keeps matching rows (values compare exactly as printed in the file), and\n182  two facts may share one `from` with different `where`: that is how you split actual from\n183  budget out of one `UNION ALL` answer. `columns` keeps only the columns you name, in order.\n184  \n185  ```json data\n186  \"dimensions\": {\n187    \"sector\":   { \"label\": \"Sector\", \"keys\": [\n188        { \"key\": \"re\", \"label\": \"Real Estate\" }, { \"key\": \"infra\", \"label\": \"Infrastructure\" } ] },\n189    \"scenario\": { \"label\": \"Kịch bản\", \"keys\": [\"Actual\", \"Budget\"] }\n190  },\n191  \"facts\": {\n192    \"sector_pnl\":  { \"from\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\",\n193                     \"where\": { \"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\" },\n194                     \"columns\": [\"sector\", \"scenario\", \"toi\", \"nii\", \"nfi\"] },\n195    \"action_plan\": [ { \"owner\": \"RBG\", \"item\": \"Đẩy CASA quý 3\" } ]\n196  }\n197  ```\n198  \n199  **A column you slice on must be declared in `data.dimensions`** — named by a measure `where`,\n200  by `filters[]`, or as a table `dim`. For a fact by reference the engine will declare it for\n201  you off the resolved rows, but only for a column holding **text in every row** (and fewer than\n202  50 members). One number anywhere makes the column measurable rather than sliceable, so a key\n203  column of period codes or years is always yours. Declare one yourself, too, whenever you want\n204  to fix the label or the slicer order: your members are kept as written and any the data also\n205  holds are appended.\n206  \n207  A short key list is only a risk on a column the engine cannot speak for. **Text** members are\n208  merged into what you wrote — declare `[\"re\", \"infra\"]` against data that also holds\n209  `general id` and the third is appended for you, silently and correctly. A column holding\n210  numbers anywhere is not merged, so its list is yours alone and a period the data holds and\n211  your `keys` miss fails the build naming that value. Check a declared *period* list against\n212  the data; a declared text list needs no checking. (The one text case that does bite: over 50\n213  members, where the column is passed over entirely and every value beyond your list is an\n214  error — the warning says so.)\n215  \n216  Declaring a dimension does not create a slicer, only `filters[]` does. Above, `sector` goes in\n217  `filters[]`; `scenario` stays declared and unexposed, which is what a query-only column wants.\n218  \n219  ### Write every value the way the evidence prints it\n220  \n221  Dimension keys, both kinds of `where`, `chart_afb.months` — all of them are compared against\n222  the column as it appears in the file. The evidence header names the columns and the preview\n223  shows the values; copy from there rather than tidying on the way in.\n224  \n225  One asymmetry is worth knowing, because it is the only place the same period has two\n226  spellings. A column of whole numbers reaches the dimension layer as a *number*, so a declared\n227  key and a `where` against it — on a measure or on a chart series — carry no trailing `.0`,\n228  written either as a number or as a string. A fact `where` filters the raw file before anything\n229  is declared, so it keeps whatever the file prints. `months` is neither: the card types it as\n230  strings, so a period on an `afb_line` axis is always quoted.\n231  \n232  ## Getting the numbers\n233  \n234  Two kinds of figure, two routes, and there is no third:\n235  \n236  | You need it to… | Route |\n237  |---|---|\n238  | **decide what to say** — is the gap material, which sector is worst, does the trend hold | `compute_measures` |\n239  | **put it on the page** | declare a measure; the report evaluates it at render time |\n240  \n241  Both speak one language, and mostly the same words. An `expr` and a `const` are written\n242  **identically** in both. An aggregate differs in one key: the spec names the evidence\n243  directly (`\"source\": \"wi_…\"`), the config names a fact and the fact names the evidence\n244  (`\"fact\": \"sector_toi\"` → `data.facts.sector_toi.from`). So the spec you reason with becomes\n245  the config you build with. Never work a figure out twice.\n246  \n247  **Read before you compute.** Genie does not hand you raw rows; it hands you the result of\n248  SQL that already did the work. A table carrying a `vs_…` column (`vs_budget_pct`,\n249  `vs_sply_abs`) **already holds that comparison** — read the column. Deriving it again spends\n250  a round-trip to produce a second answer to a question the data has answered, and a second\n251  answer is something that can disagree with the first.\n252  \n253  You also do not need to open a table to see what is in it. The evidence block says so:\n254  `<evidence id=\"wi_…\" rows=\"164\" cols=\"section,sector,rptmonth,toi_bn_vnd,…\">` lists every\n255  column, with a preview underneath.\n256  \n257  **One call, not ten.** Write the whole set with `write_file`, then call once:\n258  \n259  ```json compute_spec\n260  {\"measures\": {\n261    \"toi_act\":  {\"agg\": \"sum\", \"source\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n262                 \"where\": {\"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\"}, \"format\": \"ty1\"},\n263    \"toi_bud\":  {\"agg\": \"sum\", \"source\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n264                 \"where\": {\"section\": \"sector_budget_2026\"}, \"format\": \"ty1\"},\n265    \"toi_ach\":  {\"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\"},\n266    \"headcount\": {\"const\": 10973, \"from\": \"wi_hr_2f03\"}}}\n267  ```\n268  \n269  then `compute_measures(spec_path=\"/measures.json\")`. Every call is a round-trip; ten calls\n270  is ten of them.\n271  \n272  **Say which rows you mean.** A Genie answer is often a `UNION ALL` — one table holding\n273  actual, YTD, budget, forecast and per-product rows, told apart by a `section` or\n274  `query_section` column, and usually with a `sector = \"all\"` rollup among them. An `agg`\n275  with no `where` on that table adds budget to actual to forecast and counts the rollup\n276  twice. The result comes back with the sections and their row counts attached, so a warning\n277  in the output means read it, not ignore it.\n278  \n279  **Never copy a returned value into a file.** Not into the config, not into a draft, not into\n280  a todo. If you need it again, call `compute_measures` again with the same `spec_path` — one\n281  deterministic call. A figure retyped by hand is a figure that can be retyped wrong, and that\n282  is the one failure mode nothing downstream can catch.\n283  \n284  ## Measures\n285  \n286  Exactly one of the three shapes per measure — the card's three branches are\n287  `additionalProperties:false`, so a mixed measure fails all three as a cryptic shape error.\n288  An **aggregate** is recomputed over the rows the slicer leaves active; an **expr** over other\n289  measures follows them; a **const** is fixed. `where` pins an aggregate to fixed rows\n290  regardless of slicer state: `{\"scenario\": \"Actual\"}`, `{\"sector\": [\"re\",\"infra\"]}`. Its\n291  columns and values must be declared dimensions and declared keys. A fact's `where` shares\n292  the JSON shape and does a different job — it filters the file before anything is declared.\n293  \n294  Number format is **intl**: `,` groups thousands, `.` is the decimal point. That is the\n295  format the data arrives in — a Genie table reads `4,349.8` — and the report prints it\n296  back unchanged. Do not re-punctuate a figure on its way into the config. `ty` in\n297  `ty0/ty1/ty2` is the unit *tỷ VND*, not a locale.\n298  \n299  ### Every figure is a token (Constitution I — inviolable)\n300  \n301  Numbers reach the page only through `{{measure}}` or `{{measure|format}}`, which works\n302  anywhere text is shown. `kpi_card.value` and `driver_row` box `val` must contain a token;\n303  `insight.html`, `subtitle.text`, kpi `sub` and `delta.text` are scanned for formatted numbers\n304  (`9,972.6`, `+1.6%`, `8.5`) sitting outside one. A headline that isn't summable from fact\n305  rows is a `const` measure — still bound by a token.\n306  \n307  `const` is the last door a hand-typed number could reach the page through, so it takes\n308  exactly one kind of value: a scalar a sub-agent or `compute_measures` returned, and you can\n309  name the evidence it came from. Not a figure you worked out yourself, and not one you\n310  rounded on the way in. If it can be aggregated from the facts, it is an `agg`, not a `const`.\n311  \n312  ```text\n313  WRONG — the numbers are typed:  \"sub\": \"Đạt: 101.6% · +9.2% vs Budget\"\n314  ```\n315  \n316  RIGHT — declare them in `measures`, then reference them:\n317  \n318  ```json measures\n319  { \"toi_ach_total\":  { \"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\" },\n320    \"nii_vs_bud_pct\": { \"expr\": \"(nii_act - nii_bud) / nii_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\" } }\n321  ```\n322  ```text\n323  \"sub\": \"Đạt: {{toi_ach_total|pct1}} · {{nii_vs_bud_pct|pct1}} vs Budget\"\n324  ```\n325  \n326  Never invent a literal, never rescale (`/1000`, `*100`), never modify sub-agent SQL or data.\n327  This is why one figure cannot appear two ways in one report.\n328  \n329  ## Blocks worth a note\n330  \n331  `<strong>`/`<em>` survive in prose; everything else is escaped.\n332  \n333  **`table`** is always click-to-sort and filter-aware. Pick exactly one row mode: fact-driven\n334  (`fact` + `columns[].col`, rows auto-generate — prefer this for detail tables) or explicit\n335  (`rows`). Add `dim` to make rows hide under a slicer, and `total` with tokens for a footer\n336  that recomputes.\n337  \n338  **An action card's `body` is a list, not a paragraph.** Each entry is one labelled part —\n339  `Tại sao`, `Hành động`, `Tác động`, `Chủ trì` — and `label` is what the card renders as a\n340  heading. A single long string there is the wrong type, and would have lost the structure.\n341  \n342  ```json block_kpi_group\n343  { \"type\": \"kpi_group\", \"columns\": 3,\n344    \"cards\": [\n345      { \"label\": \"TOI 6T2026\", \"value\": \"{{toi_6m_act}}\", \"unit\": \"tỷ\",\n346        \"sub\": \"KH: {{toi_6m_bud|ty1}}\", \"cf\": \"ach_rag\",\n347        \"delta\": { \"text\": \"{{toi_vs_bud_pct|pct1}} vs KH\", \"dir\": \"positive\" },\n348        \"progress\": { \"ratio\": \"{{toi_6m_ach}}\", \"cap\": 120, \"rule\": \"ach_rag\" } } ] }\n349  ```\n350  \n351  ```json block_actions\n352  { \"type\": \"actions\",\n353    \"cards\": [\n354      { \"priority\": \"high\", \"title\": \"Tái đàm phán lãi suất MLT nhóm Real Estate\",\n355        \"body\": [\n356          { \"label\": \"Tại sao\", \"html\": \"RE đạt <strong>{{re_6m_ach|pct1}}</strong> kế hoạch.\" },\n357          { \"label\": \"Hành động\", \"items\": [\"Rà soát top 10 khách hàng RE\",\n358                                            \"Cross-sell ST Working Capital\"] },\n359          { \"label\": \"Tác động\", \"html\": \"+10 bps NIM MLT trong H2.\" } ] } ] }\n360  ```\n361  \n362  ## Charts\n363  \n364  **Charts always bind a fact** — there is no inline values/categories mode, because\n365  click-to-filter needs a real column to key the slicer on.\n366  \n367  **A series narrows its own fact with `where`**, the same clause a measure takes and against the\n368  same declared keys. So an evidence answering *\"TOI by sector by month by scenario\"* is **one**\n369  fact, and lines that differ only by scenario are `where` clauses over it — not one fact each.\n370  A pinned dimension stops following the slicer and whatever the `where` leaves alone keeps\n371  following it — so a line pinned to one scenario holds that scenario whatever the reader\n372  selects, and still narrows when they click a sector.\n373  \n374  `afb_line` draws three lines over one time axis and its shape differs from the other two:\n375  a top-level `months`, then three sibling series, all three required. Each series is **looked\n376  up by period value** — for every entry of `months` the engine takes every row whose `month_col`\n377  prints the same and totals them (`agg` changes how, default `sum`). Row order is irrelevant,\n378  nothing is padded, and a period matching no row is a gap in that line, which is how Actual\n379  stops mid-year and Forecast starts where it stopped. If several rows share a period you get\n380  their total, so `where` down to the one line you mean — a fact holding every sector draws the\n381  sector total, which is right only if that is what the chart is for.\n382  \n383  ```json chart_afb\n384  { \"kind\": \"afb_line\",\n385    \"months\": [\"202601\", \"202602\", \"202603\", \"202604\", \"202605\", \"202606\",\n386               \"202607\", \"202608\", \"202609\", \"202610\", \"202611\", \"202612\"],\n387    \"actual\":   { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n388                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"act\" } } },\n389    \"forecast\": { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n390                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"fc\" } } },\n391    \"budget\":   { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n392                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"bud\" } } },\n393    \"forecast_band\": [\"202607\", \"202612\"] }\n394  ```\n395  \n396  The repetition in that block is the shape, not an oversight: `actual`, `forecast` and `budget`\n397  are three objects and each carries its own `from`. There is no `fact` or `month_col` at the\n398  spec level to lift them to — the card shows none, and hoisting them there fails the build.\n399  The repeated `from` is exactly what lets three lines read one fact under different `where`.\n400  \n401  `months` is also the tick label, so binding a period column by reference puts period codes on\n402  the axis — the price of not retyping the numbers, and the right one. `month_col` defaults to\n403  `month`, which fits only a wide fact that already has one row per month and a column of that\n404  name (`campaign.json`).\n405  \n406  ## Filters, dimensions and conditional formats\n407  \n408  `filters: [ { \"dim\": \"sector\" } ]` builds the slicer and turns on chart cross-filtering.\n409  **A chart axis or pie slice bound to a declared dimension needs that dimension in\n410  `filters[]`** — without slicer state to toggle, the click would silently do nothing, so the\n411  lint rejects it. (If the axis isn't meant to be interactive, don't bind it to a dimension.)\n412  \n413  `conditional_formats` maps values to RAG classes, referenced by name from a KPI `cf`,\n414  progress `rule`, or cell `cf`:\n415  \n416  ```json conditional_format\n417  { \"thresholds\": [ {\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":85,\"class\":\"bad\"}, {\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":100,\"class\":\"warn\"} ],\n418    \"else_class\": \"good\" }\n419  ```\n420  \n421  `cf` and `rule` take that **name**, or an inline object — never a `{{token}}`. Which value\n422  gets tested is a separate key, `target`, and its default is the useful case: leave `target`\n423  out and the rule reads whatever field it is attached to, so one declared rule colours twenty\n424  KPI cards each by its own figure. Set `target` only when the value to test is not the value\n425  on display — a card showing tỷ but coloured by achievement percent — and then write the rule\n426  inline, since it is specific to that card. No key names a measure from inside a threshold;\n427  `target` is the only thing that chooses which number.\n428  \n429  ### One column, four places\n430  \n431  `where`, `dimensions`, `filters[]` and a chart axis all touch the same column and do four\n432  different jobs. Nearly every question that *looks* like a report-design choice is answered\n433  here, and answered the same way every time:\n434  \n435  | The column appears in… | Runs | Does | Does **not** do |\n436  |---|---|---|---|\n437  | a fact `where` | before anything is declared, on the raw file | drops non-matching rows for good — nothing downstream sees them | make the column a dimension; the rows are gone, not pinned |\n438  | a measure or series `where` | at render, against declared keys | **pins that one dimension for that one binding** — the slicer for it stops applying *here* | pin the other dimensions; those still follow the slicer |\n439  | `data.dimensions` | build | fixes label and slicer order, and lets a `where` name the column | build a slicer |\n440  | `filters[]` | build | builds the slicer and turns on chart cross-filtering | reach a fact whose rows lack that column |\n441  | a chart `x.col` / pie `name_col` | build | the axis, and the click target | survive as a *declared* dimension that `filters[]` omits — that combination fails the build. An axis on a plain column is fine and simply does not click |\n442  \n443  Two consequences are worth reading rather than deriving, because both are cheap to state and\n444  expensive to prove from the outside:\n445  \n446  **A pinned series does not go blank.** A series carrying `where: {\"sector\": \"re\"}` still shows\n447  Real Estate when the reader selects Infrastructure: for `sector`, the pin wins. So one section\n448  can hold six sector-pinned charts *and* a live `sector` slicer — the charts stay put while\n449  every unpinned binding moves. You never need a fact pre-filtered per member, or a dropped\n450  column, to get that.\n451  \n452  **A column you never mention is never a dimension.** Inference reaches only columns the config\n453  already declares or names — in `filters[]`, a measure `where` key, or a table `dim`. A\n454  `product` column sitting in a fact nobody queries by product stays an ordinary column: no keys,\n455  nothing validated against, and no way to trip the chart lint. Adding a dimension never adds a\n456  failure, so a column you are not slicing on needs no thought at all.\n457  \n458  ### Cross-filtering across breakdowns\n459  \n460  A slicer only reaches a fact whose rows carry that dimension's column, so two breakdowns\n461  co-filter only through a shared column. In order of preference:\n462  \n463  1. **One fact at the intersection grain.** Ask Genie for the metric broken down by *both*\n464     dims in a single query and emit one fact. Every measure aggregates from it, both slicers\n465     cross-filter everything, and totals reconcile.\n466  2. **Stamp the parent key** when grains differ but a hierarchy exists (product → segment):\n467     add the parent key to each child row, and picking a `sector` narrows the product fact.\n468     The reverse stays undone on purpose — a segment total can't be re-derived from one\n469     product without product-grain data.\n470  3. **Keep them independent and say so.** Two disjoint facts are two honest lenses. Note it\n471     rather than invent an intersection Genie never returned.\n472  \n473  Model at the grain you want filtered and ask Genie for that grain. Never synthesize the\n474  intersection in the config.\n475  \n476  ## Sections\n477  \n478  A section's `label` is both the eyebrow above its H2 and its sidebar link — one source, no\n479  `nav` field. `in_nav:false` hides a section; `nav_level:1` nests it.\n480  \n481  ## When build_report says ERROR\n482  \n483  Nothing was written — no report, no half-built file — and the message holds every problem\n484  found, grouped, each with its JSON path and the allowed values at that path. So it is a\n485  punch list, not a diagnosis to make.\n486  \n487  The tool's last line names the repair: `edit_file` on the same `config_path` when the list\n488  names a few keys, a fresh `write_file` when it reaches across most of the config. Either way,\n489  fix **all** the items before calling again — one fix per round trip returns the same list\n490  minus that item. Read the errors literally and group them: `where clause points at an\n491  undeclared dimension: 'scenario'` repeated across 13 measures is one missing declaration in\n492  `data.dimensions`, and *undeclared* means **add** the declaration, never delete the\n493  reference. `edit_file`'s `old_string` must match the file as it stands right now.\n494  \n495  What decides the cost here is not which repair you pick, it is how many times you repair —\n496  and that number is fixed before the first build, by how many key names you took from *The\n497  contract* and how many you supplied yourself.\n498  \n499  **After three rejections the tool stops accepting attempts.** Deliver the analysis and the\n500  figures in chat instead, and say which part of the config is blocking — an answer costs the\n501  reader less than a fourth attempt at the same class of punch-list. The count is per report,\n502  not per stage or per call site, and a build that succeeds starts it over.","duration_ms":0}}

