data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","item":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"toolu_bdrk_01WSxeDBe2S4WaXVH47zgydA","output":"  1  # CIBG Product Interpretation Framework\n  2  \n  3  ## Metadata\n  4  - Domain: cibg\n  5  - Category: products\n  6  - Tags: product-framework, interpretation, CASA, TD, lending, FX, trade-service, commentary\n  7  - Last updated: 2026-06-19\n  8  - Source: D1_Business_Rules-Metrics__CIBG_PRODUCTS.md\n  9  \n 10  ## Summary\n 11  A structured framework for interpreting CIBG product performance in business-finance analysis. For each product, defines the analytical lens: what it is, key metrics, core drivers, common distortions, and interpretation rules. Includes cross-product rules to prevent misleading conclusions.\n 12  \n 13  ## Detail\n 14  \n 15  ### How to Use This Framework\n 16  \n 17  For each product, the analyst should understand:\n 18  1. **Definition** — what the product represents commercially\n 19  2. **Primary metrics** — which KPIs assess performance\n 20  3. **Core business drivers** — what causes growth, decline, or volatility\n 21  4. **Common distortions** — what creates misleading short-term movements\n 22  5. **How to interpret changes** — what questions to ask before concluding\n 23  6. **Common analytical cuts** — which dimensions are most useful\n 24  \n 25  ### Universal Driver Framework\n 26  \n 27  All CIBG products share a common set of core drivers:\n 28  \n 29  | Driver | Deposit/Lending products | Other products (FX, Trade Service) |\n 30  | --- | --- | --- |\n 31  | Penetration (%) | % khách hàng sử dụng sản phẩm trên tổng khách hàng | % khách hàng sử dụng sản phẩm trên tổng khách hàng |\n 32  | Holding cus | Số lượng khách hàng đang nắm giữ sản phẩm | Số lượng khách hàng đang nắm giữ sản phẩm |\n 33  | Avg bal / Avg val | Số dư bình quân (Avg bal) | Giá trị giao dịch bình quân (Avg val) |\n 34  | %Margin | Biên lợi nhuận trên sản phẩm | Biên lợi nhuận trên sản phẩm |\n 35  \n 36  > **Decomposition logic:** Revenue = Holding cus × Avg bal (or val) × %Margin. Penetration drives Holding cus growth.\n 37  \n 38  ---\n 39  \n 40  ### 1. CASA (Current Account & Savings Account)\n 41  \n 42  **Definition:** Low-cost deposit balances in current/savings accounts. Treated as a balance product and key funding/relationship indicator.\n 43  \n 44  **Why it matters:**\n 45  - Supports low-cost funding\n 46  - Reflects transaction banking engagement\n 47  - Signals relationship depth and cash management quality\n 48  - Materially affects funding mix and margin economics\n 49  \n 50  **Primary metrics:** EOP bal, Avg bal, Net increase/decrease, Growth rate (MoM/QoQ/YoY), CASA share within total deposits, NTB vs existing-book CASA (by custype)\n 51  \n 52  **Core drivers:**\n 53  - Penetration (%) — tỷ lệ khách hàng CIBG có CASA trên tổng khách hàng\n 54  - Holding cus — số lượng khách hàng đang giữ CASA\n 55  - Avg bal — số dư bình quân mỗi khách hàng\n 56  - %Margin — biên lợi nhuận từ CASA (funding cost advantage)\n 57  \n 58  **Common distortions:**\n 59  - Temporary cash parking near period-end\n 60  - One-off inflows from project milestones\n 61  - Large short-lived balances from concentrated clients\n 62  - Month-end treasury/settlement timing effects\n 63  - Operational delays in outflows or internal transfers\n 64  \n 65  **Interpretation checklist:**\n 66  - Was the movement broad-based or concentrated in a few customers?\n 67  - Is the change structural or temporary timing effect?\n 68  - Did uplift come from existing customers or new business?\n 69  - Is the balance linked to a known pipeline event or one-off deal?\n 70  - Does average balance tell a different story from EOP balance?\n 71  \n 72  **Useful analytical cuts:** segment_group_cbc / segment, Sector / industry, custype (Existing vs New), area / cbc_lv1 / cbc_lv2\n 73  \n 74  > **Caution:** Do not treat all CASA growth as equally valuable. Sustainable operational CASA is more meaningful than short-lived concentration-driven balances.\n 75  \n 76  ---\n 77  \n 78  ### 2. TD (Term Deposit)\n 79  \n 80  **Definition:** Time-bound deposit balances placed for a defined tenor, usually at higher funding cost than CASA.\n 81  \n 82  **Why it matters:**\n 83  - Important funding volume source\n 84  - Supports balance-sheet growth or liquidity objectives\n 85  - More rate-sensitive than CASA\n 86  \n 87  **Primary metrics:** EOP bal, Avg bal, Net increase/decrease, Cost of funds / implied pricing, Mix by tenor, Mix by customer segment\n 88  \n 89  **Core drivers:**\n 90  - Penetration (%) — tỷ lệ khách hàng CIBG có TD trên tổng khách hàng\n 91  - Holding cus — số lượng khách hàng đang giữ TD\n 92  - Avg bal — số dư bình quân mỗi khách hàng\n 93  - %Margin — biên lợi nhuận từ TD (spread giữa lending yield và cost of TD)\n 94  \n 95  **Interpretation checklist:**\n 96  - Was growth price-led or relationship-led?\n 97  - Did balances come from new money or internal migration from CASA?\n 98  - Is the increase sustainable after campaign expiry?\n 99  - Are shorter-tenor balances driving volatility?\n100  \n101  > **Caution:** Growth in TD should not be seen as automatically positive if it materially worsens funding cost or cannibalizes CASA.\n102  \n103  ---\n104  \n105  ### 3. Lending\n106  \n107  **Definition:** Outstanding credit exposure or loan balances extended to customers.\n108  \n109  **Why it matters:**\n110  - Primary earning-asset driver\n111  - Core source of interest income\n112  - Strongly linked to risk, utilization, and portfolio quality\n113  \n114  **Primary metrics:** Outstanding balance, Average balance, Disbursement volume, Utilization / drawdown, Yield / spread, NIM contribution, New booking vs runoff\n115  \n116  **Core drivers:**\n117  - Penetration (%) — tỷ lệ khách hàng CIBG có dư nợ trên tổng khách hàng\n118  - Holding cus — số lượng khách hàng đang có dư nợ\n119  - Avg bal — dư nợ bình quân mỗi khách hàng\n120  - %Margin — biên lãi ròng (NIM/spread) trên danh mục cho vay\n121  \n122  **Common distortions:**\n123  - Backdated disbursement/booking effects\n124  - Short-term utilization spikes\n125  - Large bullet repayments\n126  - Refinancing/restructuring impacts\n127  - Product migration or portfolio transfer\n128  \n129  **Interpretation checklist:**\n130  - Was movement driven by new origination, utilization, or runoff?\n131  - Was balance change broad-based or from a few large names?\n132  - Did pricing or mix change alongside balance?\n133  - Is growth aligned with approved credit appetite and risk quality?\n134  \n135  > **Caution:** Loan growth without risk context or pricing context is incomplete. Balance growth should be interpreted together with margin and portfolio quality.\n136  \n137  ---\n138  \n139  ### 4. FX (Foreign Exchange)\n140  \n141  **Definition:** Customer foreign-exchange transactions and income generated from those flows.\n142  \n143  **Why it matters:**\n144  - Important fee and trading-related revenue source\n145  - Linked to customer trade activity and [[transaction-banking]] relationships\n146  - Sensitive to market conditions and customer flow intensity\n147  \n148  **Primary metrics:** FX income / revenue, Transaction volume, Number of active FX customers\n149  \n150  **Core drivers:**\n151  - Penetration (%) — tỷ lệ khách hàng CIBG có giao dịch FX trên tổng khách hàng\n152  - Holding cus — số lượng khách hàng active giao dịch FX\n153  - Avg val — giá trị giao dịch FX bình quân mỗi khách hàng\n154  - %Margin — biên spread/fee trên mỗi giao dịch FX\n155  \n156  **Common distortions:**\n157  - One-off large client deals\n158  - Market spikes not representative of recurring flow\n159  - Deal timing across periods\n160  - Spread compression or exceptional margin events\n161  \n162  **Interpretation checklist:**\n163  - Did revenue change because of flow volume, spread, or customer mix?\n164  - Was performance concentrated in a few clients?\n165  - Is the uplift market-driven or franchise-driven?\n166  - Did active customer penetration improve?\n167  \n168  > **Caution:** FX income volatility can be high. Strong performance in one period may not be recurring if driven by exceptional market conditions or one-off deals.\n169  \n170  ---\n171  \n172  ### 5. Trade Service (LC, LG, Collection, Cash Management)\n173  \n174  **Definition:** Products related to guarantees, letters of credit, collections, and cash management solutions.\n175  \n176  **Why it matters:**\n177  - Supports customer relationship depth\n178  - Generates fee and flow-based income\n179  - Often reinforces CASA and FX performance\n180  \n181  **Primary metrics:** Transaction volume, Fee income, Active customers, Product penetration, Balance linkage to CASA/settlement accounts\n182  \n183  **Core drivers:**\n184  - Penetration (%) — tỷ lệ khách hàng CIBG sử dụng Trade Service trên tổng khách hàng\n185  - Holding cus — số lượng khách hàng active sử dụng Trade Service\n186  - Avg val — giá trị giao dịch trade bình quân mỗi khách hàng\n187  - %Margin — biên fee/income trên mỗi giao dịch trade\n188  \n189  **Common distortions:**\n190  - Project-based trade spikes\n191  - Timing of shipment/settlement cycles\n192  - One-off documentary or guarantee issuance\n193  \n194  **Interpretation checklist:**\n195  - Is product usage deepening within existing customers?\n196  - Is activity linked to broader relationship growth?\n197  - Are fee and flow growth aligned, or is price driving the result?\n198  \n199  ---\n200  \n201  ### Cross-Product Interpretation Rules\n202  \n203  #### Rule 1: Separate structural vs temporary movements\n204  \n205  Every analysis must distinguish:\n206  - Recurring business growth\n207  - One-off transactional spikes\n208  - Timing effects\n209  - Concentration-driven changes\n210  \n211  #### Rule 2: Separate balance growth vs value growth\n212  \n213  Higher balances ≠ better economics:\n214  - CASA growth may be low quality if temporary\n215  - TD growth may worsen funding cost\n216  - Loan growth may dilute margin or increase risk\n217  \n218  #### Rule 3: Check breadth vs concentration\n219  \n220  Always assess whether results are:\n221  - Broad-based across customers and segments, or\n222  - Driven by a few names, deals, or short-lived events\n223  \n224  #### Rule 4: Connect products rather than analyzing in isolation\n225  \n226  Product reinforcement patterns:\n227  - CASA may improve with [[transaction-banking]] activation (domestic remittance)\n228  - FX may improve with overseas remittance, trade, and lending activity\n229  - Trade services often drive CASA stickiness\n230  \n231  ---\n232  \n233  ### Pre-Commentary Checklist\n234  \n235  Before writing a conclusion about ANY product, ask:\n236  1. What exactly is the KPI being discussed?\n237  2. Is the movement structural, seasonal, concentrated, or one-off?\n238  3. Which segment / sector / customer group drove the change?\n239  4. Is the observed movement commercially meaningful?\n240  5. Are there timing, pricing, accounting, or booking effects that may distort?\n241  6. Is there supporting evidence from related products or customer behavior?\n242  \n243  ---\n244  \n245  ### Commentary Guidance\n246  \n247  When explaining product performance:\n248  - Define the product briefly if needed\n249  - Describe what changed\n250  - Explain the most likely business drivers\n251  - State whether movement appears structural or temporary\n252  - Mention concentration or timing caveats\n253  - Suggest the next analytical check if confidence is limited\n254  \n255  ## See Also\n256  - [[transaction-banking]]\n257  - [[originate-to-distribution]]\n258  - [[cibg-customer-segmentation]]\n259  - [[cost-allocation]]\n260  \n261  ## Change History\n262  | Date | Change | Source |\n263  |------|--------|--------|\n264  | 2026-05-15 | Initial creation — product interpretation framework | CIBG_PRODUCTS.md |\n265  | 2026-06-19 | Standardize core drivers: Penetration, Holding cus, Avg bal/val, %Margin | User directive |","duration_ms":0}}

