data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","item":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"sa_a8896fbe","output":"101        progress        | width state ∈ good warn bad   ⊕   ratio | cap rule\n102    driver_row          type* boxes*                                    boxes ≤ 4\n103      boxes[]           val* lbl* | delta\n104    chart               type* id* spec* | title sub size ∈ sm md lg\n105    chart_pair          type* charts*                                   charts = 2\n106    table               type* columns* | fact rows dim total sortable variant ∈ default product\n107      columns[]         | label col align ∈ left right center format cf\n108      rows[]            key* cells*\n109        cells[]         v* | bold align tag ∈ up down warn neutral cf   ⊕   a string\n110      total             cells*\n111    actions             type* cards*\n112      cards[]           priority* title* body* | action_id      priority ∈ high medium\n113        body[]          label* | html items signal              signal: text* | label\n114    divider             type*\n115  \n116  spec → kind\n117    bar_group | line    kind* x* series* | y_name value_suffix legend\n118      x                 from_fact* col*\n119      series[]          from* | name type ∈ bar line line_style ∈ solid dashed dotted label\n120        from            fact* col* | agg where\n121    pie                 kind* slices* | radius value_suffix legend\n122      slices            from_fact* name_col* value_col*\n123    afb_line            kind* months* actual* forecast* budget* | forecast_band y_name\n124      actual            from*\n125      forecast          from*\n126      budget            from*\n127        from            fact* col* | month_col where\n128  \n129  format                int ty0 ty1 ty2 pct0 pct1 pct2 signed_ty0 signed_ty1 usd_mn kh raw\n130  ```\n131  \n132  Four things the card states and that are worth reading twice, because each one has cost a\n133  whole build:\n134  \n135  **Two unions, two different discriminators.** A **block** says `type`; a **chart spec** says\n136  `kind`. Writing `kind` on a block fails at every block in the config at once.\n137  \n138  **A name that fits one level up is still a rejected key.** `title` belongs to a section, a\n139  chart block and an action card — and to nothing else, so a `kpi_group` or a `table` has no\n140  title. `text` is `subtitle`'s and `delta`'s; `insight` takes `html`. `items` is an action\n141  body's; `kpi_group` and `actions` take `cards`. A section has no `subtitle` key — `subtitle`\n142  is a block you put in its `blocks`.\n143  \n144  **The card names every key there is.** When a build rejects one, the fix is on the card, not\n145  in a second guess: `change` on a kpi card is `delta: {text, dir}`; `value`/`max` on a\n146  `progress` is `ratio`/`cap`; `min`/`max`/`color` on a threshold is `op`/`value`/`class`; a\n147  format you compose yourself (`n0`) is `int`.\n148  \n149  **`format` is display only and never rescales.** A count — customers, transactions,\n150  headcount — is `int`.\n151  \n152  The card gives the keys; this gives the nesting. It is the smallest config that builds:\n153  \n154  ```json config\n155  { \"meta\": { \"label\": \"TECHCOMBANK · CIBG\", \"title\": \"CIBG — TOI 6T2026\",\n156              \"meta_line\": \"6T2026 · nguồn: Genie · BF Hub\" },\n157    \"data\": {\n158      \"dimensions\": { \"sector\": { \"label\": \"Sector\", \"keys\": [\"re\", \"infra\"] } },\n159      \"facts\": { \"sector_toi\": { \"from\": \"wi_cibg_a4c8c2c1\",\n160                                 \"where\": { \"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\" } } } },\n161    \"measures\": { \"toi_act\": { \"agg\": \"sum\", \"fact\": \"sector_toi\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n162                               \"format\": \"ty1\" } },\n163    \"filters\": [ { \"dim\": \"sector\" } ],\n164    \"sections\": [ { \"id\": \"overview\", \"label\": \"01 — Tổng quan\", \"title\": \"Tổng quan CIBG\",\n165                    \"blocks\": [ { \"type\": \"insight\",\n166                                  \"html\": \"TOI đạt <strong>{{toi_act|ty1}} tỷ</strong>.\" } ] } ] }\n167  ```\n168  \n169  ## Data\n170  \n171  `data.facts` maps `fact_id → table`, and a table has **two shapes — the shape is where the\n172  numbers came from**:\n173  \n174  | Shape | Write it as | When |\n175  |---|---|---|\n176  | **by reference** (default) | `{ \"from\": \"wi_…\", \"where\": {…}?, \"columns\": […]? }` | the numbers came from Genie — i.e. nearly always |\n177  | inline rows | `[ {col: value, …}, … ]` | only a table you composed yourself: an action plan, a footnote |\n178  \n179  By reference, the engine opens the evidence and takes the rows itself. Nothing is retyped,\n180  so nothing can be mistyped — and a fact you point at costs a line of config instead of two\n181  hundred. `where` keeps matching rows (values compare exactly as printed in the file), and\n182  two facts may share one `from` with different `where`: that is how you split actual from\n183  budget out of one `UNION ALL` answer. `columns` keeps only the columns you name, in order.\n184  \n185  ```json data\n186  \"dimensions\": {\n187    \"sector\":   { \"label\": \"Sector\", \"keys\": [\n188        { \"key\": \"re\", \"label\": \"Real Estate\" }, { \"key\": \"infra\", \"label\": \"Infrastructure\" } ] },\n189    \"scenario\": { \"label\": \"Kịch bản\", \"keys\": [\"Actual\", \"Budget\"] }\n190  },\n191  \"facts\": {\n192    \"sector_pnl\":  { \"from\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\",\n193                     \"where\": { \"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\" },\n194                     \"columns\": [\"sector\", \"scenario\", \"toi\", \"nii\", \"nfi\"] },\n195    \"action_plan\": [ { \"owner\": \"RBG\", \"item\": \"Đẩy CASA quý 3\" } ]\n196  }\n197  ```\n198  \n199  **A column you slice on must be declared in `data.dimensions`** — named by a measure `where`,\n200  by `filters[]`, or as a table `dim`. For a fact by reference the engine will declare it for\n201  you off the resolved rows, but only for a column holding **text in every row** (and fewer than\n202  50 members). One number anywhere makes the column measurable rather than sliceable, so a key\n203  column of period codes or years is always yours. Declare one yourself, too, whenever you want\n204  to fix the label or the slicer order: your members are kept as written and any the data also\n205  holds are appended.\n206  \n207  A short key list is only a risk on a column the engine cannot speak for. **Text** members are\n208  merged into what you wrote — declare `[\"re\", \"infra\"]` against data that also holds\n209  `general id` and the third is appended for you, silently and correctly. A column holding\n210  numbers anywhere is not merged, so its list is yours alone and a period the data holds and\n211  your `keys` miss fails the build naming that value. Check a declared *period* list against\n212  the data; a declared text list needs no checking. (The one text case that does bite: over 50\n213  members, where the column is passed over entirely and every value beyond your list is an\n214  error — the warning says so.)\n215  \n216  Declaring a dimension does not create a slicer, only `filters[]` does. Above, `sector` goes in\n217  `filters[]`; `scenario` stays declared and unexposed, which is what a query-only column wants.\n218  \n219  ### Write every value the way the evidence prints it\n220  \n221  Dimension keys, both kinds of `where`, `chart_afb.months` — all of them are compared against\n222  the column as it appears in the file. The evidence header names the columns and the preview\n223  shows the values; copy from there rather than tidying on the way in.\n224  \n225  One asymmetry is worth knowing, because it is the only place the same period has two\n226  spellings. A column of whole numbers reaches the dimension layer as a *number*, so a declared\n227  key and a `where` against it — on a measure or on a chart series — carry no trailing `.0`,\n228  written either as a number or as a string. A fact `where` filters the raw file before anything\n229  is declared, so it keeps whatever the file prints. `months` is neither: the card types it as\n230  strings, so a period on an `afb_line` axis is always quoted.\n231  \n232  ## Getting the numbers\n233  \n234  Two kinds of figure, two routes, and there is no third:\n235  \n236  | You need it to… | Route |\n237  |---|---|\n238  | **decide what to say** — is the gap material, which sector is worst, does the trend hold | `compute_measures` |\n239  | **put it on the page** | declare a measure; the report evaluates it at render time |\n240  \n241  Both speak one language, and mostly the same words. An `expr` and a `const` are written\n242  **identically** in both. An aggregate differs in one key: the spec names the evidence\n243  directly (`\"source\": \"wi_…\"`), the config names a fact and the fact names the evidence\n244  (`\"fact\": \"sector_toi\"` → `data.facts.sector_toi.from`). So the spec you reason with becomes\n245  the config you build with. Never work a figure out twice.\n246  \n247  **Read before you compute.** Genie does not hand you raw rows; it hands you the result of\n248  SQL that already did the work. A table carrying a `vs_…` column (`vs_budget_pct`,\n249  `vs_sply_abs`) **already holds that comparison** — read the column. Deriving it again spends\n250  a round-trip to produce a second answer to a question the data has answered, and a second\n251  answer is something that can disagree with the first.\n252  \n253  You also do not need to open a table to see what is in it. The evidence block says so:\n254  `<evidence id=\"wi_…\" rows=\"164\" cols=\"section,sector,rptmonth,toi_bn_vnd,…\">` lists every\n255  column, with a preview underneath.\n256  \n257  **One call, not ten.** Write the whole set with `write_file`, then call once:\n258  \n259  ```json compute_spec\n260  {\"measures\": {\n261    \"toi_act\":  {\"agg\": \"sum\", \"source\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n262                 \"where\": {\"section\": \"sector_monthly_actual\"}, \"format\": \"ty1\"},\n263    \"toi_bud\":  {\"agg\": \"sum\", \"source\": \"wi_cibg_7dc30c60\", \"col\": \"toi_bn_vnd\",\n264                 \"where\": {\"section\": \"sector_budget_2026\"}, \"format\": \"ty1\"},\n265    \"toi_ach\":  {\"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\"},\n266    \"headcount\": {\"const\": 10973, \"from\": \"wi_hr_2f03\"}}}\n267  ```\n268  \n269  then `compute_measures(spec_path=\"/measures.json\")`. Every call is a round-trip; ten calls\n270  is ten of them.\n271  \n272  **Say which rows you mean.** A Genie answer is often a `UNION ALL` — one table holding\n273  actual, YTD, budget, forecast and per-product rows, told apart by a `section` or\n274  `query_section` column, and usually with a `sector = \"all\"` rollup among them. An `agg`\n275  with no `where` on that table adds budget to actual to forecast and counts the rollup\n276  twice. The result comes back with the sections and their row counts attached, so a warning\n277  in the output means read it, not ignore it.\n278  \n279  **Never copy a returned value into a file.** Not into the config, not into a draft, not into\n280  a todo. If you need it again, call `compute_measures` again with the same `spec_path` — one\n281  deterministic call. A figure retyped by hand is a figure that can be retyped wrong, and that\n282  is the one failure mode nothing downstream can catch.\n283  \n284  ## Measures\n285  \n286  Exactly one of the three shapes per measure — the card's three branches are\n287  `additionalProperties:false`, so a mixed measure fails all three as a cryptic shape error.\n288  An **aggregate** is recomputed over the rows the slicer leaves active; an **expr** over other\n289  measures follows them; a **const** is fixed. `where` pins an aggregate to fixed rows\n290  regardless of slicer state: `{\"scenario\": \"Actual\"}`, `{\"sector\": [\"re\",\"infra\"]}`. Its\n291  columns and values must be declared dimensions and declared keys. A fact's `where` shares\n292  the JSON shape and does a different job — it filters the file before anything is declared.\n293  \n294  Number format is **intl**: `,` groups thousands, `.` is the decimal point. That is the\n295  format the data arrives in — a Genie table reads `4,349.8` — and the report prints it\n296  back unchanged. Do not re-punctuate a figure on its way into the config. `ty` in\n297  `ty0/ty1/ty2` is the unit *tỷ VND*, not a locale.\n298  \n299  ### Every figure is a token (Constitution I — inviolable)\n300  \n\n[Read 200 lines (lines 101-300 of 502 total). 202 lines remaining from offset 300.]","duration_ms":0}}

