data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","item":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"sa_932d3d37","output":"301  Numbers reach the page only through `{{measure}}` or `{{measure|format}}`, which works\n302  anywhere text is shown. `kpi_card.value` and `driver_row` box `val` must contain a token;\n303  `insight.html`, `subtitle.text`, kpi `sub` and `delta.text` are scanned for formatted numbers\n304  (`9,972.6`, `+1.6%`, `8.5`) sitting outside one. A headline that isn't summable from fact\n305  rows is a `const` measure — still bound by a token.\n306  \n307  `const` is the last door a hand-typed number could reach the page through, so it takes\n308  exactly one kind of value: a scalar a sub-agent or `compute_measures` returned, and you can\n309  name the evidence it came from. Not a figure you worked out yourself, and not one you\n310  rounded on the way in. If it can be aggregated from the facts, it is an `agg`, not a `const`.\n311  \n312  ```text\n313  WRONG — the numbers are typed:  \"sub\": \"Đạt: 101.6% · +9.2% vs Budget\"\n314  ```\n315  \n316  RIGHT — declare them in `measures`, then reference them:\n317  \n318  ```json measures\n319  { \"toi_ach_total\":  { \"expr\": \"toi_act / toi_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\" },\n320    \"nii_vs_bud_pct\": { \"expr\": \"(nii_act - nii_bud) / nii_bud * 100\", \"format\": \"pct1\" } }\n321  ```\n322  ```text\n323  \"sub\": \"Đạt: {{toi_ach_total|pct1}} · {{nii_vs_bud_pct|pct1}} vs Budget\"\n324  ```\n325  \n326  Never invent a literal, never rescale (`/1000`, `*100`), never modify sub-agent SQL or data.\n327  This is why one figure cannot appear two ways in one report.\n328  \n329  ## Blocks worth a note\n330  \n331  `<strong>`/`<em>` survive in prose; everything else is escaped.\n332  \n333  **`table`** is always click-to-sort and filter-aware. Pick exactly one row mode: fact-driven\n334  (`fact` + `columns[].col`, rows auto-generate — prefer this for detail tables) or explicit\n335  (`rows`). Add `dim` to make rows hide under a slicer, and `total` with tokens for a footer\n336  that recomputes.\n337  \n338  **An action card's `body` is a list, not a paragraph.** Each entry is one labelled part —\n339  `Tại sao`, `Hành động`, `Tác động`, `Chủ trì` — and `label` is what the card renders as a\n340  heading. A single long string there is the wrong type, and would have lost the structure.\n341  \n342  ```json block_kpi_group\n343  { \"type\": \"kpi_group\", \"columns\": 3,\n344    \"cards\": [\n345      { \"label\": \"TOI 6T2026\", \"value\": \"{{toi_6m_act}}\", \"unit\": \"tỷ\",\n346        \"sub\": \"KH: {{toi_6m_bud|ty1}}\", \"cf\": \"ach_rag\",\n347        \"delta\": { \"text\": \"{{toi_vs_bud_pct|pct1}} vs KH\", \"dir\": \"positive\" },\n348        \"progress\": { \"ratio\": \"{{toi_6m_ach}}\", \"cap\": 120, \"rule\": \"ach_rag\" } } ] }\n349  ```\n350  \n351  ```json block_actions\n352  { \"type\": \"actions\",\n353    \"cards\": [\n354      { \"priority\": \"high\", \"title\": \"Tái đàm phán lãi suất MLT nhóm Real Estate\",\n355        \"body\": [\n356          { \"label\": \"Tại sao\", \"html\": \"RE đạt <strong>{{re_6m_ach|pct1}}</strong> kế hoạch.\" },\n357          { \"label\": \"Hành động\", \"items\": [\"Rà soát top 10 khách hàng RE\",\n358                                            \"Cross-sell ST Working Capital\"] },\n359          { \"label\": \"Tác động\", \"html\": \"+10 bps NIM MLT trong H2.\" } ] } ] }\n360  ```\n361  \n362  ## Charts\n363  \n364  **Charts always bind a fact** — there is no inline values/categories mode, because\n365  click-to-filter needs a real column to key the slicer on.\n366  \n367  **A series narrows its own fact with `where`**, the same clause a measure takes and against the\n368  same declared keys. So an evidence answering *\"TOI by sector by month by scenario\"* is **one**\n369  fact, and lines that differ only by scenario are `where` clauses over it — not one fact each.\n370  A pinned dimension stops following the slicer and whatever the `where` leaves alone keeps\n371  following it — so a line pinned to one scenario holds that scenario whatever the reader\n372  selects, and still narrows when they click a sector.\n373  \n374  `afb_line` draws three lines over one time axis and its shape differs from the other two:\n375  a top-level `months`, then three sibling series, all three required. Each series is **looked\n376  up by period value** — for every entry of `months` the engine takes every row whose `month_col`\n377  prints the same and totals them (`agg` changes how, default `sum`). Row order is irrelevant,\n378  nothing is padded, and a period matching no row is a gap in that line, which is how Actual\n379  stops mid-year and Forecast starts where it stopped. If several rows share a period you get\n380  their total, so `where` down to the one line you mean — a fact holding every sector draws the\n381  sector total, which is right only if that is what the chart is for.\n382  \n383  ```json chart_afb\n384  { \"kind\": \"afb_line\",\n385    \"months\": [\"202601\", \"202602\", \"202603\", \"202604\", \"202605\", \"202606\",\n386               \"202607\", \"202608\", \"202609\", \"202610\", \"202611\", \"202612\"],\n387    \"actual\":   { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n388                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"act\" } } },\n389    \"forecast\": { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n390                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"fc\" } } },\n391    \"budget\":   { \"from\": { \"fact\": \"toi\", \"col\": \"value\", \"month_col\": \"rptmonth\",\n392                            \"where\": { \"data_type\": \"bud\" } } },\n393    \"forecast_band\": [\"202607\", \"202612\"] }\n394  ```\n395  \n396  The repetition in that block is the shape, not an oversight: `actual`, `forecast` and `budget`\n397  are three objects and each carries its own `from`. There is no `fact` or `month_col` at the\n398  spec level to lift them to — the card shows none, and hoisting them there fails the build.\n399  The repeated `from` is exactly what lets three lines read one fact under different `where`.\n400  \n401  `months` is also the tick label, so binding a period column by reference puts period codes on\n402  the axis — the price of not retyping the numbers, and the right one. `month_col` defaults to\n403  `month`, which fits only a wide fact that already has one row per month and a column of that\n404  name (`campaign.json`).\n405  \n406  ## Filters, dimensions and conditional formats\n407  \n408  `filters: [ { \"dim\": \"sector\" } ]` builds the slicer and turns on chart cross-filtering.\n409  **A chart axis or pie slice bound to a declared dimension needs that dimension in\n410  `filters[]`** — without slicer state to toggle, the click would silently do nothing, so the\n411  lint rejects it. (If the axis isn't meant to be interactive, don't bind it to a dimension.)\n412  \n413  `conditional_formats` maps values to RAG classes, referenced by name from a KPI `cf`,\n414  progress `rule`, or cell `cf`:\n415  \n416  ```json conditional_format\n417  { \"thresholds\": [ {\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":85,\"class\":\"bad\"}, {\"op\":\"lt\",\"value\":100,\"class\":\"warn\"} ],\n418    \"else_class\": \"good\" }\n419  ```\n420  \n421  `cf` and `rule` take that **name**, or an inline object — never a `{{token}}`. Which value\n422  gets tested is a separate key, `target`, and its default is the useful case: leave `target`\n423  out and the rule reads whatever field it is attached to, so one declared rule colours twenty\n424  KPI cards each by its own figure. Set `target` only when the value to test is not the value\n425  on display — a card showing tỷ but coloured by achievement percent — and then write the rule\n426  inline, since it is specific to that card. No key names a measure from inside a threshold;\n427  `target` is the only thing that chooses which number.\n428  \n429  ### One column, four places\n430  \n431  `where`, `dimensions`, `filters[]` and a chart axis all touch the same column and do four\n432  different jobs. Nearly every question that *looks* like a report-design choice is answered\n433  here, and answered the same way every time:\n434  \n435  | The column appears in… | Runs | Does | Does **not** do |\n436  |---|---|---|---|\n437  | a fact `where` | before anything is declared, on the raw file | drops non-matching rows for good — nothing downstream sees them | make the column a dimension; the rows are gone, not pinned |\n438  | a measure or series `where` | at render, against declared keys | **pins that one dimension for that one binding** — the slicer for it stops applying *here* | pin the other dimensions; those still follow the slicer |\n439  | `data.dimensions` | build | fixes label and slicer order, and lets a `where` name the column | build a slicer |\n440  | `filters[]` | build | builds the slicer and turns on chart cross-filtering | reach a fact whose rows lack that column |\n441  | a chart `x.col` / pie `name_col` | build | the axis, and the click target | survive as a *declared* dimension that `filters[]` omits — that combination fails the build. An axis on a plain column is fine and simply does not click |\n442  \n443  Two consequences are worth reading rather than deriving, because both are cheap to state and\n444  expensive to prove from the outside:\n445  \n446  **A pinned series does not go blank.** A series carrying `where: {\"sector\": \"re\"}` still shows\n447  Real Estate when the reader selects Infrastructure: for `sector`, the pin wins. So one section\n448  can hold six sector-pinned charts *and* a live `sector` slicer — the charts stay put while\n449  every unpinned binding moves. You never need a fact pre-filtered per member, or a dropped\n450  column, to get that.\n451  \n452  **A column you never mention is never a dimension.** Inference reaches only columns the config\n453  already declares or names — in `filters[]`, a measure `where` key, or a table `dim`. A\n454  `product` column sitting in a fact nobody queries by product stays an ordinary column: no keys,\n455  nothing validated against, and no way to trip the chart lint. Adding a dimension never adds a\n456  failure, so a column you are not slicing on needs no thought at all.\n457  \n458  ### Cross-filtering across breakdowns\n459  \n460  A slicer only reaches a fact whose rows carry that dimension's column, so two breakdowns\n461  co-filter only through a shared column. In order of preference:\n462  \n463  1. **One fact at the intersection grain.** Ask Genie for the metric broken down by *both*\n464     dims in a single query and emit one fact. Every measure aggregates from it, both slicers\n465     cross-filter everything, and totals reconcile.\n466  2. **Stamp the parent key** when grains differ but a hierarchy exists (product → segment):\n467     add the parent key to each child row, and picking a `sector` narrows the product fact.\n468     The reverse stays undone on purpose — a segment total can't be re-derived from one\n469     product without product-grain data.\n470  3. **Keep them independent and say so.** Two disjoint facts are two honest lenses. Note it\n471     rather than invent an intersection Genie never returned.\n472  \n473  Model at the grain you want filtered and ask Genie for that grain. Never synthesize the\n474  intersection in the config.\n475  \n476  ## Sections\n477  \n478  A section's `label` is both the eyebrow above its H2 and its sidebar link — one source, no\n479  `nav` field. `in_nav:false` hides a section; `nav_level:1` nests it.\n480  \n481  ## When build_report says ERROR\n482  \n483  Nothing was written — no report, no half-built file — and the message holds every problem\n484  found, grouped, each with its JSON path and the allowed values at that path. So it is a\n485  punch list, not a diagnosis to make.\n486  \n487  The tool's last line names the repair: `edit_file` on the same `config_path` when the list\n488  names a few keys, a fresh `write_file` when it reaches across most of the config. Either way,\n489  fix **all** the items before calling again — one fix per round trip returns the same list\n490  minus that item. Read the errors literally and group them: `where clause points at an\n491  undeclared dimension: 'scenario'` repeated across 13 measures is one missing declaration in\n492  `data.dimensions`, and *undeclared* means **add** the declaration, never delete the\n493  reference. `edit_file`'s `old_string` must match the file as it stands right now.\n494  \n495  What decides the cost here is not which repair you pick, it is how many times you repair —\n496  and that number is fixed before the first build, by how many key names you took from *The\n497  contract* and how many you supplied yourself.\n498  \n499  **After three rejections the tool stops accepting attempts.** Deliver the analysis and the\n500  figures in chat instead, and say which part of the config is blocking — an answer costs the\n501  reader less than a fourth attempt at the same class of punch-list. The count is per report,\n502  not per stage or per call site, and a build that succeeds starts it over.","duration_ms":0}}

