data: {"type":"response.output_item.done","item":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"sa_7368d795","output":"  1  ---\n  2  name: report-builder-v2\n  3  description: Builds an interactive, filterable HTML report (PowerBI-style) from a compact JSON ReportConfig via the build_report tool. Use when the user asks for a report, a báo cáo, an executive summary as a document, a dashboard-style HTML deliverable, or a shareable link to analysis results.\n  4  ---\n  5  \n  6  # Report Builder v2\n  7  \n  8  You write a **JSON `ReportConfig`**; `build_report` renders it into a self-contained\n  9  interactive report (sidebar, slicers, cross-filtering charts, RAG, sortable tables,\n 10  light/dark themes). The template owns all HTML/CSS/JS and every colour — you never write\n 11  markup and never choose a theme. Your job is content, data and relationships.\n 12  \n 13  ## Workflow\n 14  \n 15  1. **Open a preset in `assets/examples/` and read it fully** — it is your template.\n 16     - `performance.json` — segment P&L. Two facts by reference out of one evidence, split\n 17       by `where`. One slicer, `insight` · `subtitle` · `kpi_group` · `driver_row` · `chart` ·\n 18       `chart_pair` · `table` (fact-driven) · `actions`, RAG. Start here.\n 19     - `campaign.json` — marketing, and the one config holding **both** fact shapes: Genie's\n 20       channel table by reference, a hand-composed monthly table inline. Adds `divider`, an\n 21       `afb_line` with `forecast_band`, and a `table` in explicit `rows` mode.\n 22     - `segment_scenario.json` — a long fact (`scenario` column: Actual/Budget/SPLY) with a\n 23       declaration-only dimension. Copy this one whenever your data has a scenario or\n 24       metric column rather than one column per scenario.\n 25  2. **Point `data.facts` at the evidence the sub-agent saved** — `{\"from\": \"wi_…\"}`, not the\n 26     rows. See *Data*.\n 27  3. **Work out what the report says with `compute_measures`, not in your head** — see\n 28     *Getting the numbers*.\n 29  4. **Declare a measure for every figure you will show**, then bind them with `{{measure}}`.\n 30  5. **Write the config with `write_file` to `/report_config.json`, then call\n 31     `build_report(config_path=\"/report_config.json\", filename=…)`.**\n 32  \n 33  Adapt the preset and send it. `build_report` validates before rendering and returns **every**\n 34  problem it found in one message, each with its location — so submitting a config is how you\n 35  check it. Re-reading your own JSON to be certain first costs more than the call does, and\n 36  finds less.\n 37  \n 38  ### Two stages, when one emission would be too big\n 39  \n 40  A whole config is one `write_file` argument, and a large one has been cut off mid-JSON by\n 41  the 64,000-token output ceiling — nothing saved, nothing to edit, the whole emission lost.\n 42  Splitting the submission avoids that, and gets the half that carries the numbers checked\n 43  before you write the half that shows them.\n 44  \n 45  Pick a short `session` name (`cibg-h1`), then:\n 46  \n 47  1. `write_file` the **semantic model** — `meta`, `data`, `measures`, `filters`, and nothing\n 48     else — to `/report-sessions/<session>/model.json`. That exact path is where the model is\n 49     sealed and where stage 2 looks for it; it is also what survives a compaction.\n 50  2. `build_report(stage=\"model\", session=\"<session>\", config_path=\"/report-sessions/<session>/model.json\")`.\n 51     On success it hands back every identifier stage 2 may bind to. Do not start writing\n 52     sections until this passes — they bind to it.\n 53  3. `write_file` the **presentation** — `sections`, plus `conditional_formats` and\n 54     `format_defaults` if you use them — to a file of your choosing.\n 55  4. `build_report(stage=\"sections\", session=\"<session>\", config_path=…, filename=…)`. This\n 56     builds the report.\n 57  \n 58  Stage 2 takes every section at once: there is no per-section call, because a section is only\n 59  checkable against the sections around it. Stage 2 may reference only what the sealed model\n 60  declares — a fact, measure or dimension that is not in it is rejected by name.\n 61  \n 62  Two stages do not buy two budgets: the three-rejection rule below counts the report, not the\n 63  stage. And the one-shot route is unchanged — omit `stage` and send the whole config when it\n 64  is small enough to emit safely.\n 65  \n 66  ## The contract\n 67  \n 68  Every object below is closed: a key that is not on this card is rejected, and the error\n 69  prints the allowed list for that exact path. Guessing is therefore never cheaper than\n 70  looking — the card is the whole contract, and `contracts/report-config.schema.json` says\n 71  the same thing in 29 KB with no lookup order.\n 72  \n 73  Read it as: `key*` required · after `|` optional · `∈` the complete set of allowed values ·\n 74  `⊕` two exclusive shapes · `→` the key that tells the union's members apart.\n 75  \n 76  ```text\n 77  config                meta* data* sections* | measures filters conditional_formats\n 78  \n 79  meta                  title* | label doc_title meta_line lang brand footer\n 80  data                  facts* | dimensions\n 81    facts{}             from* | where columns\n 82    dimensions{}        | label keys\n 83  filters[]             dim* | header\n 84  measures{}            one shape per measure:\n 85    aggregate           agg* fact* col* | where format\n 86    expr                expr* | format\n 87    const               const* from* | format\n 88                        agg ∈ sum avg min max count last first\n 89  conditional_formats{} thresholds* | target else_class\n 90    thresholds[]        op* value* class* | value2\n 91                        op ∈ lt lte gt gte eq between\n 92                        class, else_class ∈ good warn bad up down neutral\n 93  sections[]            id* title* blocks* | label in_nav nav_level ∈ 0 1\n 94  \n 95  blocks[] → type\n 96    insight             type* html*\n 97    subtitle            type* text* | margin_top\n 98    kpi_group           type* cards* | columns ∈ 2 3\n 99      cards[]           label* value* | unit sub cf delta progress\n100        delta           text* | dir ∈ positive negative neutral\n\n[Read 100 lines (lines 1-100 of 502 total). 402 lines remaining from offset 100.]","duration_ms":0}}

